index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cfb155925b10ce54d863c58c5aeb280ec2b0e2b2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-002-timestamps-passed-in-by-the-caller-not-read-from-the-clock-in-the-domain.md
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-timestamps-passed-in-by-the-caller-not-read-from-the-clock-in-the-domain
+type: decision-log
+title: "Timestamps passed in by the caller, not read from the clock in the domain"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The app needs to show how old a task is and what changed recently, so each task needs a `createdAt` and
+an `updatedAt`. The `determinism` and `r3-no-clock-or-randomness` directives forbid reading the wall
+clock anywhere under `src/domain/`, which rules out stamping these fields with `new Date().toISOString()`
+or `Date.now()` from inside `task.ts` or `list.ts`. `dl-001` faced the same constraint for task ids and
+resolved it by taking the id as a parameter instead of generating it; timestamps follow the same shape,
+except the list cannot generate "the next time" the way it generates "the next id" from a counter, so the
+value has to come from outside the domain on every call, not just once at construction.
+
+## Decision
+
+`Task` gains `createdAt` and `updatedAt`, both ISO-8601 date-time strings. `createTask` takes a third
+parameter, `now`, and sets both fields to it. `completeTask`, `reopenTask`, `renameTask`, `addTag` and
+`removeTag` each take a `now` parameter too and set `updatedAt` to it, leaving `createdAt` untouched.
+
+`TaskList`'s mutating methods — `add`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag` — each gain a
+required `now` parameter and thread it through to the matching domain function. `now` is accepted as
+given, the same way `id` is: the list does not parse or validate it, trusting the caller (the app, via
+`src/index.ts`) to supply the current time. A call that is refused (completing an already-done task,
+renaming onto a duplicate title) does not touch `updatedAt`, since the domain function returns an error
+before building a new task. Tagging or untagging with a tag the task already has (or does not have)
+still counts as a change and still updates `updatedAt`, consistent with `addTag`/`removeTag` already
+returning a new object in that case before this change.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Every mutating `TaskList` method now takes one more required argument; every existing call site in
+  `test/list.test.ts` needed updating, and so will every caller in the app.
+- `src/domain/` stays a pure function of its inputs, satisfying `r3-no-clock-or-randomness`; nothing here
+  prevents `src/index.ts` or the app from reading the real clock when it calls these methods.
+- `now` is unchecked input; a caller that passes a non-ISO-8601 string or an out-of-order time (e.g. an
+  `updatedAt` before the task's `createdAt`) gets no error from this package. Acceptable for an
+  in-memory, single-process list whose only caller is the app itself; would need revisiting if this
+  package is ever called from less trusted code.
+</content>
